<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page4-best-response" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Finding Nash Equilibria Using Best Response</title>

  <p>
    So far we have found Nash equilibria two ways: by inspecting profiles one
    at a time, and by eliminating dominated strategies and hoping the theorems
    from the last page let us keep what survives. This page gives us the method
    we will use for the rest of the course. It is faster than inspection, it
    never loses an equilibrium the way weak elimination can, and once you have
    the hang of it you can read the answer off a matrix in about a minute.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-8">
    <title>Section 3.8, Finding Nash Equilibria Using Best Response</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.8.</alert> It is two pages and mostly notation.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Two notational points before we go on, since the book writes things out
    longhand and we are going to compress them.
  </p>

  <p>
    First, recall <m>s_{-i}</m> from the first page of this chapter. It means
    the strategies of everybody except player <m>i</m>. On page 1 we only ever
    used it with a star, as <m>s^*_{-i}</m>, because we were always talking
    about the other players sitting at their equilibrium strategies. Here we
    need the plain version too: <m>s_{-i}</m> is just some fixed choice by
    everyone else, not necessarily an equilibrium one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Second, the book writes player 1's best response set as
    <m>B_1(s_2, \ldots, s_n)</m>, and for a general player <m>i</m> it would
    have to write <m>B_i(s_1, \ldots, s_{i-1}, s_{i+1}, \ldots, s_n)</m>, with
    an awkward gap where <m>s_i</m> would go. We will write
    <m>B_i(s_{-i})</m> instead. It means exactly the same thing, and it is the
    same compression we used on page 1 when we shortened the definition of a
    Nash equilibrium. Do not go looking for <m>B_i(s_{-i})</m> in the
    textbook; that notation is ours, not theirs.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now the definition itself. The key thing to notice is that a best response
    is not defined for a player on their own. It is only defined once you have
    fixed what everyone else is doing. Given that fixed choice
    <m>s_{-i}</m>, player <m>i</m>'s best responses are the strategies that
    give player <m>i</m> the highest payoff available against it. There may be
    more than one, since two strategies can tie for the highest payoff. That is
    why <m>B_i(s_{-i})</m> is a <em>set</em>, and why the book calls the
    mapping a best response <term>correspondence</term> rather than a
    function.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-words">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In words, what is a best response for player <m>i</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Hold the other players' strategies fixed. Player <m>i</m>'s best
            responses to that are the strategies giving player <m>i</m> the
            highest payoff available against it, and there may be more than one
            if some of them tie.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Both halves matter: a best response only makes sense
            relative to a fixed <m>s_{-i}</m>, and there can be several of
            them at once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Hold the other players' strategies fixed. Player <m>i</m>'s best
            response is <em>the</em> strategy giving player <m>i</m> the
            highest payoff against it.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Almost, but the word <q>the</q> is doing damage. Nothing stops two
            strategies from tying for the highest payoff, in which case both
            are best responses. You will see exactly this happen in the next
            section, and it is the reason <m>B_i(s_{-i})</m> is a set.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The strategy that gives player <m>i</m> the highest payoff no
            matter what the other players do.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That is a dominant strategy, which is a much stronger condition and
            usually does not exist. A best response is always a response
            <em>to something</em>; change what the other players are doing and
            player <m>i</m>'s best response can change with it.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The reason we care is the sentence at the end of Section 3.8, which is
    worth stating on its own because it is what every method on this page
    rests on. A strategy profile
    <m>s^* = (s_1^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium <em>if and only
    if</em> every player's own piece of it is a best response to what everyone
    else is doing:
  </p>

  <p>
    <m>s_i^* \in B_i(s^*_{-i})</m> for every player <m>i = 1, \ldots, n</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is not a new fact so much as the definition of a Nash equilibrium
    read in a different order. Saying that player <m>i</m> cannot improve by
    deviating from <m>s_i^*</m> is the same as saying <m>s_i^*</m> is already
    as good as anything else available against <m>s^*_{-i}</m>, which is what
    being a best response means. What is new is that it turns finding
    equilibria into a checklist: work out each player's best responses
    separately, then look for the profiles where every player's condition holds
    at once.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-nash">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In words, how does best response connect to Nash equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when every player's
            strategy <m>s_i^*</m> is a best response to the other players'
            strategies <m>s^*_{-i}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and note that it has to hold for <em>every</em> player at
            the same time. One player being happy is not enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when every player's
            strategy <m>s_i^*</m> is a best response to <m>s_{-i}</m> for
            every possible choice <m>s_{-i}</m> by the other players.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is the dominant strategy condition again, and it is much
            stronger than what a Nash equilibrium requires. The Nash condition
            only asks about one particular <m>s_{-i}</m>, namely
            <m>s^*_{-i}</m>. This is the same distinction the checkpoint on
            page 1 was drawing, now in best response language.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when at least one player's
            strategy <m>s_i^*</m> is a best response to <m>s^*_{-i}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not enough. If only some players are best responding, the ones who
            are not can improve their payoff by switching, and that is exactly
            what a Nash equilibrium forbids. Every player's condition has to
            hold simultaneously.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-9">
    <title>Section 3.9, Big Monkey and Little Monkey 5</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.9.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the same matrix you have now seen in Chapter 2 and on the last
      page, so you can spend your attention on the boxes rather than the game.
      Pay attention to how the boxes are drawn, because your homework will ask
      you to produce them and will explicitly ask you <em>not</em> to write out
      your reasoning in words.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here is the matrix once more, unboxed, so you can work along.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-bmlm-best-response">
    <title>Big Monkey and Little Monkey in normal form, Big Monkey first</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>ww</cell>
        <cell>wc</cell>
        <cell>cw</cell>
        <cell>cc</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>w</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>c</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Let's do one of Little Monkey's best responses carefully, because it is the
    interesting one. Suppose Big Monkey plays <m>c</m>. To find Little Monkey's
    best responses to that, we hold <m>c</m> fixed and look only at the
    <m>c</m> row. Her payoffs are the second entries, so reading across we get
    <m>4, 3, 4, 3</m>. The highest is <m>4</m>, and it occurs twice, under
    <m>ww</m> and under <m>cw</m>. So <em>both</em> of those are best
    responses, and we box her <m>4</m> in each of those two cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    That tie is not a quirk of this example, it is the whole reason for the
    word <q>correspondence.</q> Little Monkey's best response to <m>c</m> is a
    set with two things in it, so there is no way to write it as a function
    that returns one strategy. The book points out that Big Monkey's best
    response correspondence, by contrast, happens to be a function here, since
    he never ties.
  </p>

  <p>
    Big Monkey's direction works the same way with the roles of rows and
    columns swapped. His strategies are the rows, so to find his best response
    you fix one of Little Monkey's <em>columns</em> and read down it, comparing
    his payoffs, which are the first entries. Against <m>ww</m> he compares
    <m>0</m> and <m>4</m>, so he climbs, and we box the <m>4</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-box-big-monkey">
    <statement>
      <p>
        To find Big Monkey's best response to Little Monkey's strategy
        <m>cw</m>, what do you do, and what do you box?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look down the <m>cw</m> column, compare Big Monkey's payoffs
            <m>9</m> and <m>4</m>, and box the <m>9</m> in the <m>w</m> row.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Fixing Little Monkey's strategy means fixing a column,
            and Big Monkey's payoffs are the first entries. He waits, so the
            <m>9</m> gets boxed.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look across the <m>w</m> row, compare Big Monkey's payoffs
            <m>0, 0, 9, 9</m>, and box the two <m>9</m>s.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This compares Big Monkey's payoff against four different things
            Little Monkey might do, which is not a best response question at
            all. A best response requires the other player's strategy to be
            held fixed, and here that means staying inside a single column.
            Reading across a row is how you find <em>Little Monkey's</em> best
            responses.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Look down the <m>cw</m> column, compare the payoffs <m>1</m> and
            <m>4</m>, and box the <m>4</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right column, wrong entries. Those are Little Monkey's payoffs, the
            second numbers in each pair. Big Monkey's are the first numbers, so
            the comparison is <m>9</m> against <m>4</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Once every box is drawn, the payoff is immediate. A cell is a Nash
    equilibrium exactly when <em>every</em> entry in it is boxed, because that
    is what our condition <m>s_i^* \in B_i(s^*_{-i})</m> for all <m>i</m>
    looks like on a matrix. A cell where only some entries are boxed means at
    least one player is not best responding, and that player would rather
    switch.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-all-boxed">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Draw all the boxes for the matrix above, then use them. Which of these
        cells is a Nash equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(c, ww)</m>, with payoffs <m>(4, 4)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. In the <m>ww</m> column Big Monkey's <m>4</m> beats his
            <m>0</m>, so it is boxed. In the <m>c</m> row Little Monkey's
            <m>4</m> ties for her highest, so it is boxed too. Both entries
            boxed, so it is a Nash equilibrium, and it is the threat
            equilibrium from the last page.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(c, wc)</m>, with payoffs <m>(5, 3)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Big Monkey's <m>5</m> is indeed boxed, and it is the largest payoff
            he can get anywhere in this matrix. But look at Little Monkey. In
            the <m>c</m> row her payoffs are <m>4, 3, 4, 3</m>, so her <m>3</m>
            here is not boxed and she would rather switch to <m>ww</m> or
            <m>cw</m>. A big payoff for one player is not what makes a cell an
            equilibrium; every entry has to be boxed.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(c, cw)</m>, with payoffs <m>(4, 4)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Little Monkey's <m>4</m> is boxed here, since it ties for her
            highest in the <m>c</m> row. But in the <m>cw</m> column Big
            Monkey's <m>4</m> loses to the <m>9</m> above it, so his entry is
            not boxed and he would rather wait. Compare this with
            <m>(c, ww)</m>, which has the same payoffs <m>(4, 4)</m> but is an
            equilibrium. The payoffs alone cannot tell you; the boxes can.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-10">
    <title>Section 3.10, Water Pollution 2</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.10.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the same technique with three players instead of two, and there
      is nothing new to learn beyond the bookkeeping. It is worth reading
      closely anyway, because this is the exact format your homework will use.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    One thing about the three player case is worth saying out loud, because it
    is the only part of the method that is genuinely different and it is easy
    to get wrong. With three players the game is drawn as two matrices, one for
    each of player 3's strategies. Players 1 and 2 work exactly as before,
    inside whichever matrix you are looking at: player 1 reads down columns,
    player 2 reads across rows, both staying in a single table.
  </p>

  <p>
    Player 3 is the exception. Fixing the strategies of players 1 and 2 means
    fixing a <em>cell position</em>, and player 3's two options are the two
    tables. So you compare the third entry of the same cell in the top table
    against the third entry of that cell in the bottom table, and box the
    larger. That comparison runs between the tables rather than inside one of
    them. As always, a profile is a Nash equilibrium when all three entries in
    its cell are boxed.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-11">
    <title>Section 3.11, Cournot Duopoly</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.11.</alert> Read it for the idea rather than the
      algebra; you will not be asked to reproduce this computation.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    This section closes a loop we opened at the end of page 2. When we did the
    Tobacco Market we found ourselves taking partial derivatives and setting
    them to zero, and it was not entirely clear what we were computing. Now it
    is: we were finding best responses. In a game with finitely many
    strategies you find a best response by comparing entries in a column, and
    in a game where strategies are real numbers you find it with calculus, but
    it is the same question either way.
  </p>

  <p>
    The picture is the thing to take away. Each firm's best response is a
    function of the other firm's quantity, so each one graphs as a curve, and a
    Nash equilibrium is a point where both players are simultaneously best
    responding, which is a point where the curves cross. That is the same
    condition <m>s_i^* \in B_i(s^*_{-i})</m> for all <m>i</m> that we have
    been checking with boxes all page, drawn in the plane instead of in a
    matrix. Boxes work for finite games; the definition itself works for
    everything.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-4" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.4" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
